Amiberry: eject and insert the PCMCIA card over the IPC socket.

WHAT IT IS FOR

anxnet.device installs card.resource removal and insertion callbacks
(src/netdev/netdev_pcmcia.c, pc_on_removed and pc_on_inserted) and nothing
had ever fired them, because no emulator could take a PCMCIA network card
out of the socket while the guest was running.

Amiberry can, and already implements the whole engine: pcmcia_card_check()
in src/gayle.cpp compares changed_prefs against currprefs every frame and
calls freepcmcia() or initpcmcia() itself.  What is missing is any headless
way to reach it.  cfgfile.cpp can only ever set `inserted` TRUE -- there is
no else branch -- and IPC SET_CONFIG has a hardcoded option whitelist that
does not include the slot.  The only route that works is IPC LOAD_CONFIG
with a second .uae lacking `inserted=true`, and that is destructive: it runs
discard_prefs() + default_prefs() before parsing, which tears down the mount
list and kills the guest's DH0:.  Measured, and it happens even when the
config handed to it is unchanged, so it is not about PCMCIA at all.  A guest
with no drive cannot report what it saw, so no harness can be built on it.

This adds one SET_CONFIG option that flips the flag the existing engine is
already watching, leaving currprefs alone:

    SET_CONFIG pcmcia_inserted 0        eject
    SET_CONFIG pcmcia_inserted 1        insert

USED BY

    tests/tools/run-cardeject.sh

APPLIES TO

    Amiberry master @ 149e9aa2.  Both added includes are required; neither
    arrives transitively.

    git -C <amiberry> apply tools/patches/amiberry/amiberry-ipc-pcmcia-inserted.diff
    cmake --build <amiberry>/build --parallel 4

    PUT A NUMBER ON --parallel.  Bare `--parallel` passes -j with no argument,
    which is unlimited make jobs: it took the 8-core lab host to load 264,
    OOM-killed its own compiler and wedged sshd for every user on the machine
    for about fifteen minutes.

    Rebuilding in place swaps the binary under any emulator already running
    from it.  To build beside one instead, compile the single object with
    `make -f CMakeFiles/amiberry.dir/build.make <obj>` -- the TOP-LEVEL
    makefile silently no-ops on that path, returning 0 with no output, which
    looks exactly like a patch that did not apply -- then relink through a
    copy of CMakeFiles/amiberry.dir/link.txt with the output name changed.
    A bridged build needs `setcap cap_net_admin,cap_net_raw=eip` on it.

THE PROTOCOL IS TAB SEPARATED

    ProcessCommand() splits the line on a tab, not on spaces:

        'SET_CONFIG\tpcmcia_inserted\t1\n'  ->  b'OK\n'
        'SET_CONFIG pcmcia_inserted 1\n'    ->  b'ERROR\tUnknown command:
                                                  SET_CONFIG PCMCIA_INSERTED 1\n'
        'SET_CONFIG\tnonsense\t1\n'         ->  b'ERROR\tUnknown option: nonsense\n'

    A space-joined command arrives as one token and the reply upper-cases it,
    which is what makes that error so hard to read.  Otherwise it is as plain
    as it looks: newline terminated, no length prefix, no JSON, one recv()
    gets the whole reply, and success begins with the literal OK.

STATUS

    Verified.  Two full eject and reinsert cycles in one boot on an A1200 with
    ne2000_pcmcia bridged: SET_CONFIG answered OK both ways, the emulator
    logged `inserted=0` then `inserted=1`, and the GUEST then logged
    `PCMCIA NE2000 IO configured` with nothing driving it, which is
    pc_on_inserted() reconfiguring a card it had just been handed.
    `FILESYS: was not initialized.` count was 0 and the guest kept writing to
    DH0: afterwards, so the LOAD_CONFIG collateral damage is gone.

    The log markers are `inserted=0` and `inserted=1`.  There is no
    `freepcmcia` or `initpcmcia` in any log -- those are static C function
    names, not log text -- so a harness must not grep for them.

    WHAT IT FOUND.  After reinsertion the guest reconfigures the card but the
    SANA-II interface stays offline and down; the driver does not bring itself
    back online.  It was online with a working ping before the eject.  That is
    the defect this patch existed to expose, and tests/tools/run-cardeject.sh
    gates on it.

THIS PATCH IS LOCAL AND STAYS LOCAL.  It is not to be sent to Amiberry or
to any repository this project does not own.

diff --git a/src/osdep/amiberry_ipc_socket.cpp b/src/osdep/amiberry_ipc_socket.cpp
index 8d2a356e..7981b7bc 100644
--- a/src/osdep/amiberry_ipc_socket.cpp
+++ b/src/osdep/amiberry_ipc_socket.cpp
@@ -23,6 +23,8 @@
 #include "gui/gui_handling.h"
 #include "newcpu.h"
 #include "blitter.h"
+#include "rommgr.h"
+#include "autoconf.h"
 #ifdef DEBUGGER
 #include "debug.h"
 #endif
@@ -592,6 +594,27 @@ static std::string HandleSetConfig(const std::vector<std::string>& args)
 			changed_prefs.ntscmode = (optval == "true" || optval == "1");
 			set_config_changed();
 		}
+		// PCMCIA slot: eject and insert the configured card at runtime.
+		else if (optname == "pcmcia_inserted") {
+			const bool want = (optval == "true" || optval == "1");
+			bool found = false;
+			for (int i = 0; i < MAX_EXPANSION_BOARDS; i++) {
+				struct boardromconfig *brc = &changed_prefs.expansionboard[i];
+				if (!brc->device_type)
+					continue;
+				const struct expansionromtype *ert = get_device_expansion_rom(brc->device_type);
+				if (!ert || !(ert->deviceflags & EXPANSIONTYPE_PCMCIA))
+					continue;
+				found = true;
+				if (brc->roms[0].inserted != want) {
+					brc->roms[0].inserted = want;
+					set_config_changed();
+				}
+				break;
+			}
+			if (!found)
+				return make_response(false, {"No PCMCIA board configured"});
+		}
 		// Input options
 		else if (optname == "tablet_mode") {
 			int tablet_mode;